<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Morgan Shuster</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1877">1877</a>–<a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as William Morgan Shuster</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Morgan Shuster was an American lawyer and financial administrator appointed treasurer-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> by the Majlis in 1911 to reorganise the country's finances. His energetic reforms, including a treasury gendarmerie, alarmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and a Russian ultimatum in November 1911 forced his dismissal and the closure of the Majlis. He described the episode in The Strangling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> (1912).</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/morgan-shuster.jpg" alt="Morgan Shuster" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clinedinst · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_William_Morgan_Shuster.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>William Morgan Shuster was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, on 23 February 1877, the only son of William Shuster Jr., a prominent Washington attorney, and Caroline von Tagen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>. He studied at George Washington University and its law school. He then became a customs collector for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> government, serving with the Military Government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in 1899 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. In 1906 he was appointed Secretary of Public Instruction in the Insular Government of the Philippine Islands and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-commission">Philippine Commission</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Recommended by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> government to the Iranian minister in Washington, Shuster was engaged by the second Majlis to put the finances of the constitutional government in order. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> was burdened by debts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajars</a> owed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which had divided the country into spheres of influence under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-russian-convention">Anglo-Russian Convention</a> of 1907. Shuster oversaw the creation of a gendarmerie of 12,000 men to collect taxes. When he moved to seize the assets of Shu'a al-Saltaneh, the shah's brother and an ally of Russian aims, Russia landed troops at Bandar Anzali and demanded an apology.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> accused him of breaking the 1907 convention by sending gendarmes into the zone its army occupied and arresting Russian subjects. When the Majlis refused to dismiss him, Russian forces occupied northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>; under Russian and British pressure the regent expelled Shuster in December 1911 against the parliament's will, and the Majlis was suspended.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Back in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> he wrote The Strangling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> by the Century Company in 1912 and dedicated to the Persian people. It attacks British and Russian influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and has been praised as an eyewitness account, notably for its chapter on tax collection from payment in kind to tax farming. It was later translated into Persian. From 1915 Shuster headed the Century publishing house, which he led through mergers with D. Appleton &amp; Company in 1933 and F. S. Crofts in 1947; he became chairman in 1952. He died in New York City on 26 May 1960, when the firm was known as Appleton-Century-Crofts. His papers are in the Library of Congress.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Morgan Shuster” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Foreign pressure continued: the Anglo-Russian Convention of 1907 divided Iran into Russian and British spheres of influence, and in 1911 a Russian ultimatum forced the dismissal of the American financial adviser <b>Morgan Shuster</b> and the closure of the Majlis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.9,-77.04&amp;year=1930" aria-label="Washington, D.C. on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2640 769 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Morgan Shuster"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3090 1039h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3090 1039h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.9,-77.04&amp;year=1930">What was here in 1930? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
